---
import Layout from '../../studio/layouts/Layout.astro';
import { mailto, studio } from '../../apps/registry';

const title = 'Privacy Policy — Paperdot';
const description = 'Privacy policy for Paperdot (com.yanerapps.paperdot), a daily journal and planner for Android by Yaner Apps.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="prose">
    <nav aria-label="Paperdot navigation" class="product-nav">
      <a href="/privacy">Privacy by app</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> Paperdot
      <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span><a href="/paperdot/support">Support</a><span aria-hidden="true"> · </span><a href="/paperdot/terms">Terms</a>
    </nav>
    <div class="prose-header">
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="lede">Paperdot for Android (<code>com.yanerapps.paperdot</code>)</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <span>Effective: from Paperdot&rsquo;s release on Google Play</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>Updated: 22 Nov 2026</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      This policy explains what Paperdot does with your data. Paperdot is made by Yaner Apps, an independent
      developer studio ("we", "us"). It applies to the Paperdot Android application and to this website.
    </p>

    <div class="card">
      <p><strong>In short:</strong> Paperdot works fully on your device with no account. Your journal stays on
      your device unless you turn on <strong>Paperdot Sync</strong>, which is optional. With Sync, you sign in
      with Google and your journal is <strong>end-to-end encrypted</strong> on your device before it is
      uploaded: we store it but cannot read it. Paperdot reads your calendar only to show events next to your
      logs, and never saves them. It shows no ads. Google Play processes purchases, and we never see your
      payment details. We never sell your personal information.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>1. Data that stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
      The following is stored in the app's private storage:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your journal: entries, logs, collections, trackers and their history</li>
      <li>Reminders you set on entries</li>
      <li>App settings, including app lock, the calendars you chose to show and the backup reminder</li>
      <li>Your Pro status, as a copy signed with a key that stays in your device's secure keystore</li>
      <li>With Sync on: your sync data key, protected by a key in the device's secure keystore</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Without Sync, none of it is sent to us. You can delete entries, collections and trackers inside the app
      at any time. Uninstalling Paperdot removes everything stored on the device.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Paperdot Sync (optional)</h2>
    <p>
      Paperdot Sync keeps your journal the same on your devices. It is off until you turn it on in
      Profile &rsaquo; Sync, needs a Google sign-in, and needs a Paperdot Sync subscription to upload changes.
    </p>
    <h3>What you give us when you sign in</h3>
    <p>
      Signing in uses Google Sign-In and Firebase Authentication, a Google service we use to run accounts.
      Google shares your <strong>name, email address and profile picture</strong> with Paperdot, and Firebase
      gives your account a random user ID. We use these only to run your account: to tell your devices apart
      from other people's, to show you which account you are signed in with, and to answer you if you write
      to us.
    </p>
    <h3>What is uploaded, and what we can see</h3>
    <p>
      Before anything leaves your device, Paperdot encrypts each entry, collection, tracker and synced setting
      with a key made on your device (XChaCha20-Poly1305). That key is itself locked with your
      <strong>sync passphrase</strong> and your <strong>recovery key</strong>, which never leave your devices.
      We cannot read your journal, and neither can Google. What we store, under your user ID, is:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your encrypted records, each with a random record ID, its kind (for example "entry"), a timestamp
      used to merge changes, a deleted flag, a format version and the time it reached the server</li>
      <li>Your encrypted key document: your data key, locked by your passphrase and by your recovery key</li>
      <li>Your subscription status: whether Sync is active and when it ends</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The kinds, timestamps and counts are visible to us, so we can see roughly how much you write and when,
      but never what. If you forget your sync passphrase and lose your recovery key, we cannot recover your
      synced journal; the copy on your devices is not affected.
    </p>
    <h3>Where it is stored</h3>
    <p>
      Sync runs on Google Firebase (Cloud Firestore and Cloud Functions), in data centers in the United
      States. Google processes this data for us as a processor under the
      <a href="https://firebase.google.com/terms/data-processing-terms" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Firebase Data Processing and Security Terms</a>.
      Our server code keeps logs for up to 30 days to run and fix the service; they may contain your user ID and
      a shortened, one-way fingerprint of a purchase, never your journal or a full purchase token.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Subscriptions: Pro and Sync</h2>
    <p>
      Paperdot Pro and Paperdot Sync are auto-renewing subscriptions sold and billed entirely by Google Play.
      Google Play takes the payment, and your payment method and billing details stay with Google. We never
      receive your card number, billing address or other payment information. Google Play gives us order
      records for our sales in the Play Console, such as the order number, plan, price and date, which we use
      only for accounting, refunds and support.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Pro</strong> is checked on your device: the app asks Google Play whether you have it and keeps a
      signed copy of the answer, so Pro keeps working for up to 7 days offline. This copy is excluded from
      Android cloud backup and device-to-device transfer.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Sync</strong> is checked by our server, so that it works on every device you sign in to. When you
      subscribe, the app sends the purchase's token to our server, which asks Google Play for the purchase's
      status and links the purchase to your user ID. Google Play also notifies our server when a subscription
      renews, is cancelled, expires or is refunded. We store, for each Sync purchase: the purchase token, the
      product, its status, when it ends, and the user ID it belongs to. We keep this for as long as the
      subscription exists and as Google Play's records require, including after you delete your account, so
      that a subscription you keep paying for can follow you to a new account.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can cancel either subscription at any time in Google Play. When one ends, nothing in your journal is
      changed or deleted; Sync simply stops uploading, and you can still download and delete your synced data.
      Refunds and changes of payment method are handled by Google Play. Google's handling of payment data is
      governed by the
      <a href="https://payments.google.com/payments/apis-secure/u/0/get_legal_document?ldo=0&amp;ldt=privacynotice" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google Payments Privacy Notice</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Permissions</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Permission</th>
            <th>What Paperdot does with it</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Calendar (read only, optional)</td>
            <td>Shows events from the calendars you choose next to your daily and monthly logs. Events are read when a page is shown and are never stored in your journal, backups, exports, search or sync. Paperdot cannot create or change calendar events</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Notifications (optional)</td>
            <td>Shows the reminders you set, the backup reminder and, with Sync on, a notice when sync needs your attention</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Run at startup</td>
            <td>Puts your reminders back after the device restarts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Biometrics</td>
            <td>Lets app lock ask Android to confirm it is you, with your fingerprint, face or screen lock. Android checks it; Paperdot only learns whether it succeeded</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Network access</td>
            <td>Used by Google Play Billing, to show prices and confirm subscriptions, and by Sync when you turn it on</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Paperdot does not ask for your location, contacts, camera, microphone or files. Text you share into
      Paperdot from another app becomes a journal entry on your device.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Data sent to third parties</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Recipient</th>
            <th>What is sent</th>
            <th>Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Google Play Billing</td>
            <td>The plan you chose, your purchase, and an identifier the app derives on your device with a one-way hash</td>
            <td>To sell Pro and Sync, confirm that you have them, and help Google detect fraud</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Google Sign-In and Firebase (only with Sync)</td>
            <td>Your Google account's name, email and profile picture; your encrypted records and key document; your Sync purchase</td>
            <td>To run your account and store your encrypted journal; Google acts as our processor for Firebase</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Your journal, reminders, calendar events and settings are never sent anywhere in readable form. Paperdot
      shows no ads, starts no advertising, analytics or crash-reporting SDK, and does not use your advertising
      ID.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Backups and exports</h2>
    <p>
      Paperdot can write files to places you choose: a full backup (<code>.paperdot.json</code>), a Markdown
      export and, with Pro, a PDF export. These files contain your journal in plain text. They are created only
      when you ask for them, they are not uploaded anywhere by Paperdot, and once they leave the app they are
      yours to protect. Sharing an export through Android's share sheet sends it to the app you pick.
    </p>
    <p>
      Android's own cloud backup may include your journal and settings if you have enabled it on your device;
      app lock, calendar choices, your Pro status, your sync data key and this device's sync identity are left
      out of it. That backup is managed by Google under your Google account, not by us.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Notifications and the widget</h2>
    <p>
      Reminders and the home screen widget can show the text of your entries, so anyone who can see your screen
      can see them. App lock hides Paperdot's preview in recent apps but does not hide notifications or the
      widget. You can hide sensitive notification content on the lock screen in Android's notification
      settings.
    </p>

    <h2>8. What we do not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No account unless you turn on Sync, and nothing that needs one except Sync</li>
      <li>No readable copy of your journal on our servers, ever</li>
      <li>No ads, analytics or crash-reporting SDKs</li>
      <li>No location permission and no use of your location</li>
      <li>No sale of personal information</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>9. Children</h2>
    <p>
      Paperdot is not directed to children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect data from children.
      Without Sync, a child's journal stays on their device like anyone else's. Sync requires a Google account
      and is subject to Google's age requirements for it.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Data retention and deletion</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>On your device:</strong> your journal stays until you delete it or uninstall the app.</li>
      <li><strong>Synced data:</strong> your encrypted records and key document stay until you delete them or
      your account. If your Sync subscription has ended, we may delete your synced data 180 days after it
      ended; we will tell you in the app 30 days and 7 days before. Your journal on your devices is never
      affected.</li>
      <li><strong>Deleting your account:</strong> in the app, open Profile &rsaquo; Sync &rsaquo; Delete account.
      This deletes your encrypted records, your key document, your subscription status and your account right
      away. If you no longer have the app, see <a href="/paperdot/delete-account">how to delete your Paperdot
      account</a>. Deleting your account does not cancel a subscription: cancel it in Google Play.</li>
      <li><strong>Purchase records</strong> are kept as described in section 3. Server logs are deleted after 30
      days. Backups of our database, if any, roll over within 7 days.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>11. Security</h2>
    <p>
      Your journal is kept in the app's private storage, which other apps cannot read on a non-rooted device.
      App lock adds a check with your fingerprint, face or screen lock each time you come back to the app.
      Synced data is end-to-end encrypted as described in section 2, and travels over HTTPS; only your own
      account can read or delete its data on the server. Backup and export files are not encrypted, so keep
      them somewhere safe.
    </p>

    <h2>12. Additional information for the EEA, UK and Switzerland</h2>
    <p>
      This section gives the information required by the EU and UK General Data Protection Regulation and
      the Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection.
    </p>

    <h3>Controller</h3>
    <p>
      The controller for the processing described in this policy is Yaner Yang, publishing as Yaner Apps,
      reachable at <a href={mailto()}>{studio.email}</a>. Google acts as our processor for Firebase, and as an
      independent controller for Google Play and Google Sign-In, under its own policies.
    </p>

    <h3>Purposes and legal bases</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Purpose</th>
            <th>Data</th>
            <th>Legal basis</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Providing Pro or Sync you subscribed to</td>
            <td>Your Google Play purchase status and token, the hashed identifier sent with a purchase, and the order records Google Play provides</td>
            <td>Necessary to perform the contract you entered into when subscribing (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Running your Sync account and storing your encrypted journal</td>
            <td>Your name, email, profile picture and user ID; your encrypted records and key document; their timestamps</td>
            <td>Necessary to perform the contract (Art. 6(1)(b))</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Keeping the service running and secure</td>
            <td>Server logs with your user ID, kept 30 days</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests in operating a reliable, secure service (Art. 6(1)(f))</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Answering your emails</td>
            <td>Your email address and your message</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests in responding to you (Art. 6(1)(f)); we delete the correspondence once the matter is closed, and within 12 months at the latest</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Data that stays on your device (section 1) is not processed by us at all. You are not required to
      provide any personal data; Sync is optional. We make no decisions based solely on automated processing.
    </p>

    <h3>International transfers</h3>
    <p>
      Synced data and purchase data are processed by Google in the United States. Google relies on the EU-U.S.
      Data Privacy Framework, its UK Extension and the Swiss-U.S. Data Privacy Framework, and on Standard
      Contractual Clauses where needed (see
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy/frameworks" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google's data transfer frameworks</a>).
    </p>

    <h3>Your rights under the GDPR</h3>
    <p>
      You have the right to access, rectify and erase your personal data, to restrict or object to its
      processing, and to data portability. You also have the right to lodge a complaint with a data
      protection supervisory authority, in particular in the country where you live or work; in the UK this
      is the Information Commissioner's Office, and in Switzerland the Federal Data Protection and
      Information Commissioner. Section 13 explains how to exercise these rights.
    </p>

    <h2>13. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Depending on where you live, you may have rights to access, correct, delete, or port your personal
      data. You can exercise most of them in the app: edit or delete entries, export a backup or Markdown
      file to take your data elsewhere, and delete your Sync account. If you need help, write to
      <a href={mailto()}>{studio.email}</a> and we will respond within 30 days.
    </p>

    <h2>14. Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the updated version is published on this page with a new "updated" date.
      Material changes affecting how data is shared will also be noted in the app's release notes.
    </p>

    <h2>15. Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Yaner Apps &mdash; <a href={mailto()}>{studio.email}</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
